   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( y = f(x) \\) be the particular solution to the differential equation \\( \\frac{dy}{dx} = 2\\left(1 + y^{2}\\right) \\) with \\( f(1) = 0 \\). The solution exists on a largest open interval \\( (a, b) \\) containing \\( x = 1 \\). Find \\( b \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Add",
         [
            "Divide",
            "Pi",
            4
         ],
         1
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Separate and antidifferentiate: \\( \\arctan y = 2 x + C \\), so \\( y = \\tan(2 x + C) \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Multiply",
            2,
            "x"
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "At \\( x = 1 \\): \\( C = \\arctan(0) - 2 = -2 \\).",
         "mathjson": -2
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "The tangent is undefined where \\( 2 x + C = \\pm \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\), which gives the neighbours \\( x = 1 - \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\) and \\( x = \\frac{\\pi}{4} + 1 \\) of \\( x = 1 \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
            1,
            [
               "Negate",
               [
                  "Divide",
                  "Pi",
                  4
               ]
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "The interval containing \\( x = 1 \\) is \\( \\left(1 - \\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{4} + 1\\right) \\), so \\( b = \\frac{\\pi}{4} + 1 \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
